      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function",
      "problem": "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $g(2)=-2$ and $g(3)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-2,1],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=1$ and $g(4)=-3.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $g(4)=-3$ and $g(5)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,6],$ we have $g(5)=1$ and $g(6)=0.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $g(6)=0$ and $g(7)=2.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-1$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,4]$  and $[4,5].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-1$ has at least three solutions on $[2,7].$",
